.TH "CSSCONTAINERRULE.CONTAINERQUERY" "3JS" "May 11, 2026" "JavaScript" "JavaScript Reference Manual"
.SH NAME
CSSContainerRule.containerQuery \- CSSContainerRule: containerQuery
property
.SH SYNOPSIS
The read\-only \f[B]\f[CB]containerQuery\f[B]\f[R] property of the
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] interface represents the query part of the
container condition for a container rule that only defines one container
condition.
If there are multiple container conditions, the value is set to the
empty string.
.SH VALUE
A string that contains the query part of the container condition defined
in a container rule, but only if it has just one container condition
defined.
Note that the value may not be identical to the original string, as
normalizations such as removing whitespace may happen.
.PP
If no query is defined, or if the rule defines multiple container
conditions, this is the empty string (\f[CR]\(dq\(dq\f[R]).
.SH DESCRIPTION
This property reflects the value of the query part of the container
condition in a corresponding \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] at\-rule that has
just one container condition.
.PP
For example, the value of \f[CR]containerQuery\f[R] for the
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] below is \f[CR](width >= 700px)\f[R]:
.IP
.EX
\f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] >= 700px) {
  \f[I]/* Styles */\f[R]
}
.EE
.RS
.PP
\f[B]Note:\f[R] The \f[CR]containerQuery\f[R] value has been superseded
by \f[CR]CSSContainerRule.conditions\f[R], which should be used in
supporting browsers.
Browsers that do not support \f[CR]conditions\f[R] cannot parse
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] definitions with multiple container conditions.
.RE
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic usage
The example below defines a \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] rule with a single
container condition, and displays the properties of the associated
\f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R].
The CSS is very similar to that in the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] example
Creating named container contexts.
.SS HTML
First, we define the HTML for a \f[CR]card\f[R] contained within a
\f[CR]post\f[R].
These are represented by two nested \f[CR]<div>\f[R] elements.
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqpost\(dq>
  <\f[B]div\f[R] class=\(dqcard\(dq>
    <\f[B]h2\f[R]>Card title</\f[B]h2\f[R]>
    <\f[B]p\f[R]>Card content</\f[B]p\f[R]>
  </\f[B]div\f[R]>
</\f[B]div\f[R]>
.EE
.SS CSS
The CSS for the container element specifies the type of the container
along with a name.
The card has a default font size, which is overridden for the
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] named \f[CR]sidebar\f[R] if its width is
greater than or equal to \f[CR]700px\f[R].
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]style\f[R] id=\(dqexample\-styles\(dq>
  .post {
    \f[B]container\-type\f[R]: inline\-size;
    \f[B]container\-name\f[R]: sidebar;
  }

  \f[I]/* Default heading styles for the card title */\f[R]
  .card h2 {
    \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 1em;
  }

  \f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] >= 700px) {
    .card {
      \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 2em;
    }
  }
</\f[B]style\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The code below gets the \f[CR]HTMLStyleElement\f[R] associated with the
example using its \f[CR]id\f[R], and then uses its \f[CR]sheet\f[R]
property to get the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R].
From the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R] we get the set of \f[CR]cssRules\f[R]
added to the sheet.
Since we added the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] as the third rule above, we
can access the associated \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] using the third
entry (index \(lq2\(rq) in the \f[CR]cssRules\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleStylesheet = document.getElementById(\(dqexample\-styles\(dq).sheet;
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleRules = exampleStylesheet.cssRules;
\f[B]const\f[R] containerRule = exampleRules[2]; \f[I]// a CSSContainerRule representing the container rule.\f[R]
.EE
.PP
We then use \f[CR]containerRule\f[R] to log the query of the container
condition.
If \f[CR]CSSContainerRule.conditions\f[R] is supported in the browser,
we show the name and query from that as well.
.IP
.EX
log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.containerQuery: \(dq${containerRule.containerQuery}\(dq\(ga);

\f[B]if\f[R] (\(dqconditions\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] CSSContainerRule.prototype) {
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions:\(dq);
  containerRule.conditions.forEach((item) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] jsonString = JSON.stringify(item);
    log(\(ga  ${jsonString}\(ga);
  });
}
.EE
.SS Results
The example output is shown below.
The log section lists the query of the only container condition using
\f[CR]containerQuery\f[R].
It also shows the name and query using the \f[CR]conditions\f[R]
property, if supported.
.PP
The text in the card \f[CR]<div>\f[R] should double in size as the page
width reaches \f[CR]700px\f[R], and halve as it goes below
\f[CR]700px\f[R] again.
.SS Multiple container conditions
The example below is almost exactly the same as the previous example
except that the CSS specifies multiple container conditions.
.PP
Note that we\(cqve hidden the HTML because it is the same as in the
previous example.
.SS CSS
The card has a default font size, which is overridden for the
\f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] named \f[CR]sidebar\f[R] if its width is
greater than or equal to \f[CR]700px\f[R] or if the container has the
name \f[CR]other\-name\f[R].
Note that this condition is contrived to demonstrate the effect of
multiple conditions (it does not affect the behavior of the example).
.IP
.EX
<\f[B]style\f[R] id=\(dqexample\-styles\(dq>
  .post {
    \f[B]container\-type\f[R]: inline\-size;
    \f[B]container\-name\f[R]: sidebar;
  }

  \f[I]/* Default heading styles for the card title */\f[R]
  .card h2 {
    \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 1em;
  }

  \f[B]\(atcontainer\f[R] sidebar (\f[B]width\f[R] >= 700px), other\-name {
    .card {
      \f[B]font\-size\f[R]: 2em;
    }
  }
</\f[B]style\f[R]>
.EE
.SS JavaScript
The code below gets the \f[CR]HTMLStyleElement\f[R] associated with the
example using its \f[CR]id\f[R], and then uses its \f[CR]sheet\f[R]
property to get the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R].
From the \f[CR]StyleSheet\f[R] we get the set of \f[CR]cssRules\f[R]
added to the sheet.
Since we added the \f[CR]\(atcontainer\f[R] as the third rule above, we
can access the associated \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] using the third
entry (index \(lq2\(rq) in the \f[CR]cssRules\f[R].
.IP
.EX
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleStylesheet = document.getElementById(\(dqexample\-styles\(dq).sheet;
\f[B]const\f[R] exampleRules = exampleStylesheet.cssRules;
\f[B]const\f[R] containerRule = exampleRules[2]; \f[I]// a CSSContainerRule representing the container rule.\f[R]
.EE
.PP
The code is slightly different from the previous case because if
multiple container conditions are not supported by the browser, the
\f[CR]containerRule\f[R] will be \f[CR]undefined\f[R].
We therefore only log the value of \f[CR]containerQuery\f[R] if the
browser supports multiple container conditions \(em it will be the empty
string.
.IP
.EX
\f[B]if\f[R] (!containerRule) {
  \f[I]// Browser doesn\(aqt support multiple container conditions\f[R]
  log(
    \(dqNo CSSContainerRule was created. This browser doesn\(aqt support \(atcontainer with multiple conditions.\(dq,
  );
} \f[B]else\f[R] {
  log(\(gaCSSContainerRule.containerQuery: \(dq${containerRule.containerQuery}\(dq\(ga);
}

\f[B]if\f[R] (\(dqconditions\(dq \f[B]in\f[R] CSSContainerRule.prototype) {
  log(\(dqCSSContainerRule.conditions:\(dq);
  containerRule.conditions.forEach((item) \f[B]=>\f[R] {
    \f[B]const\f[R] jsonString = JSON.stringify(item);
    log(\(ga  ${jsonString}\(ga);
  });
}
.EE
.PP
See Feature testing in \f[CR]CSSContainerRule\f[R] for more
information/examples.
.SS Results
The example output is shown below.
Note that the rule doesn\(cqt exist at all if the browser doesn\(cqt
support multiple container conditions.
If it does, then the value of \f[CR]containerQuery\f[R] is the empty
string.
